# GAME BREAKDOWN: Miami Dolphins @ Minnesota Vikings
### Week 4, 2026 Season | Scheduled 10/04/2026, 4:05 PM ET

**EVIDENCE NOTICE:** This game has not been played. The evidence package for this matchup is labeled as a bootstrap package, but unlike a true prior-season bootstrap, the underlying team and player data here is genuine, current 2026 in-season evidence — both teams have actually played three real 2026 games apiece (Miami 0-3, against Las Vegas, San Francisco, and Kansas City; Minnesota 3-0, against Green Bay, Chicago, and Tampa Bay). These two teams have not played each other this season, and this is not a division game, so there is no real head-to-head evidence to draw on anywhere in this report. Every statistic below reflects each team's actual three-game 2026 sample, which is thin by definition this early in a season — sample size is flagged throughout, and 2025 season-long figures are cited only where they add genuine information not visible in the small 2026 sample.

---

## 1. PREGAME BRIEFING

Miami is 0-3, averaging 12.0 points per game (31st) on offense while allowing 28.7 points per game (28th) on defense — a team still searching for its footing on both sides of the ball. Minnesota is 3-0 with a far stranger profile: its offense ranks dead last in the NFL in total yards (255.3/gm, 32nd), passing yards (154.0/gm, 32nd), and first downs (11.0/gm, 32nd), yet the team is unbeaten because its defense has been outstanding — 13.7 points allowed per game (2nd), 85.7 rush yards allowed per game (4th), and league-best third-down defense at 22.7% allowed (1st).

Notably, all three of Miami's opponents to date graded as top-tier scoring defenses this season — a genuinely difficult opening slate that complicates any read of Miami's offense as simply "bad" this early. Whether Minnesota offers any real relief from that trend, or instead represents the toughest defensive test yet, is one of the central questions this report works through below.

There is no meeting between these two teams this season and no division history to draw on (this is not a division game). Broadly, this profiles as a defensively-oriented, lower-volume game shaped far more by which unit's defense imposes itself than by either offense's raw talent — Minnesota's takeaway-heavy, situationally dominant defense against Miami's offense that has shown flashes of explosiveness but has yet to find consistency, set against a Minnesota offense that has proven capable of scoring in bunches against lesser competition despite ugly yardage totals. The deeper layers of that broad picture — and where it actually resolves — are built out below.

