## 4. MATCHUP INTELLIGENCE

### Quarterbacks

**Minnesota's starter is genuinely unsettled, and this matters.** Kyler Murray's own depth-chart entry (dated 2026-09-29, five days before kickoff) lists him at pos_rank 1, ahead of Carson Wentz at pos_rank 2. But through three games, the actual snap distribution tells a different story: Wentz played 83% of snaps in Week 1 and 100% in Week 2, while Murray appeared for only 17% of Week 1's snaps and did not appear at all in Week 2, before returning to 100% of snaps in Week 3. The likeliest read is that Murray was the incumbent starter, was hurt or limited early in Week 1, and Wentz filled in through Week 2 before Murray reclaimed the job in Week 3 — consistent with the current depth chart listing him at QB1. Per Football Intel's Current Starter standard, the most current signal (depth chart) should be weighted over the fact that Wentz has more total 2026 usage to date. **Treat Murray as the more likely Week 4 starter, at MEDIUM confidence** given the injury-return framing and the current depth-chart signal, while noting real uncertainty given no validated injury report exists for this game (Section 2).

**Kyler Murray (MIN):** Across his two games (one of them a token 5-attempt appearance), Murray has 93.0 pass yds/gm, 52.94% completion, and 1 total passing touchdown — a thin, injury-influenced sample. His full Week 3 workload alone (168 yards, 15/29, 1 TD, 29 attempts) is more representative of a full-time role. This is a steep step down from his 2025 season-long form: *2025: 192.4 pass yds/gm, 68.32% completion, 1.2 pass TD/gm across 5 games as a full-time starter.* No home/road split data exists yet for Murray. Confidence on his true current form: LOW, given the tiny and partially injury-affected sample.

**Carson Wentz (MIN, likely backup):** 138.0 pass yds/gm, 58.97% completion, 3 total passing touchdowns and zero interceptions across his two appearances — efficient if unspectacular, and clean ball security. His lone home game (Week 1 vs. Green Bay, a middle-12 pass defense that game) produced 133 pass yards, 3 touchdowns, and a 123.5 rating. That represents a real decline from his own 2025 form: *2025: 243.2 pass yds/gm, 65.09% completion, across 5 starts.* If Wentz ends up playing instead of Murray, expect a passing operation that leans on ball security and touchdown efficiency over raw volume.

**Malik Willis (MIA):** The clear, entrenched starter (100% snaps in all three games, depth-chart pos_rank 1). His season line — 209.0 pass yds/gm (20th), 56.98% completion (30th), just 1 passing touchdown through three games (31st) — reflects a passer who has been busy (28.7 attempts/gm, 24th) but largely unproductive, with one real positive signal in yards-per-completion (12.8, 7th) suggesting some explosive-play capacity even amid the overall struggles. His road-specific average (the relevant side this week, MIA is the visitor) is actually a touch better than his season mark: 208.5 pass yds/gm across two road games, 58.0% completion. No road-specific sample exists yet against a bottom-10 pass defense (his own career splits only cover middle-12 and top-10 road matchups) — so the specific combination this week's matchup calls for (Minnesota ranks 26th in pass defense, bottom-10) isn't directly available in his career log; fall back to the season and road averages above. His three opponents this season have graded progressively tougher by pass defense: Las Vegas (15th), San Francisco (11th), and Kansas City (3rd) — a real, escalating gauntlet, not merely bad luck against any one team. Willis's role last season was a minimal backup one (4 mop-up appearances, 8.75 attempts/gm), so there is effectively no meaningful 2025 comparison for his current full-time starting workload. **Willis has a significant, well-supported blitz problem**: 39.3% completion and -0.374 EPA/play against the blitz (n=28) compared to 56.7% completion and +0.025 EPA/play when not blitzed (n=67) — a real, sample-supported split (MEDIUM-HIGH confidence) worth monitoring given Minnesota's defense generates pressure at the league's highest rate (4.7 sacks/gm, 1st).

Depth behind Willis: Brady Cook (0 MIA games, but a real prior-team track record — 147.8 pass yds/gm across 5 games in 2025) and Kyle McCord (no data anywhere in the evidence package, a blank slate) round out the room; neither projects to play barring a Willis injury, for which there is no validated report either way.

### Running Backs

**De'Von Achane (MIA):** The clear lead back (44.2% rush share, 14.1% target share, 55.7% snap share). His season averages — 42.33 rush yds/gm, 16.33 rec yds/gm — mark a real decline from 2025's 84.38 rush yds/gm. The efficiency data explains why: his yards-after-contact average has cratered to 1.45 yds/carry this season from 3.02 in 2025, his yards-before-contact average is actually up slightly (2.1 vs. 2.65 — so blocking hasn't been the problem), and his broken tackles per game have fallen to exactly zero from 1.38 last year. This reads as a real loss of explosiveness after contact, not a scheme or blocking issue. His road average (the relevant side this week) is actually notably better than his season mark — 55.0 rush yds/gm across two road games — and his middle-12-tier road average (the exact bucket this week's matchup calls for, given Minnesota's RB-specific run defense ranks 18th) sits at the same 55.0 rush yds/gm, 3.5 yards per carry. Context worth adding: Miami's RB corps as a whole has actually held fairly steady against middle-12 run defenses this season (63.5 rush yds/gm, n=2) relative to its season average — suggesting the group-level projection isn't collapsing purely from matchup difficulty; the concern is specifically Achane's individual per-touch efficiency. His one genuinely bad game came in Week 3 against Kansas City's top-10 run defense (2nd-ranked) — just 17 yards on 3 carries with a 5% snap share, a marked outlier that also happened to coincide with a much heavier workload for Ollie Gordon II that same game (17 carries, 41 yards, 1 TD, 84% snap share) — worth watching for signs of a lingering issue. Confidence on Achane's efficiency decline: MEDIUM-HIGH (the 2025-vs-2026 gap is real and consistent across three separate efficiency metrics).

**Ollie Gordon II (MIA):** The clear complementary piece (33.9% rush share, 36.7% snap share), with his own efficiency metrics equally modest (1.0 yd/carry after contact, 1.33 before contact) — the shared struggles point toward a broader Miami run-blocking issue rather than an Achane-specific problem. His depth-chart listing (pos_rank 2, with Jaylen Wright oddly listed at pos_rank 1 despite minimal usage — 6.5% rush share, 6% snaps) is a clear mismatch between the depth chart and actual role; treat Achane and Gordon as the real committee regardless of the nominal chart order.

**Aaron Jones (MIN):** The clear starter (61.2% rush share, 50.0% red-zone carry share). Season average of 67.67 rush yds/gm (12th among backs) is a real improvement over his 2025 pace of 45.67 rush yds/gm — a genuine, positive year-over-year shift worth noting for a 30-something veteran back. His contact-efficiency split (1.46 yds after contact, 2.69 before contact) suggests more of his production is currently coming from blocking than from individual power/elusiveness — the inverse relationship from Miami's backs. His lone home game this season (Week 1 vs. Green Bay, a bottom-10 run defense) produced 40 yards on 12 carries and a touchdown; no home sample yet exists against a middle-12 run defense (Miami's RB-specific defense ranks 18th, the tier this week's matchup calls for), so fall back to his season average and home average above. Notably, his best individual outing by raw yardage (58 yards, 17 carries) came in Week 3 against Tampa Bay's 2nd-ranked run defense — a real sign he can still produce against stout competition. Jordan Mason, who split early-season work with Jones (44.1% rush share in a big Week 1 workload, 59 yards on 15 carries against Green Bay's 30th-ranked run defense that week), has not played since and has fallen to pos_rank 4 on the current depth chart — worth noting as a name who has faded from the rotation.

### Receivers and Tight Ends

**Malik Washington (MIA):** The clear leading target (27.1% target share, 85.7% snap share, depth-chart WR1). Season average of 50.67 rec yds/gm marks a dramatic role increase from 2025, when he played a complementary 14.1% target-share role and averaged just 18.65 rec yds/gm — a real, meaningful year-over-year change in offensive standing. His road average (relevant this week) is 48.0 rec yds/gm across two games; his career splits don't yet include a bottom-10-tier road sample (Minnesota's WR defense ranks 25th, bottom-10), so fall back to the season and road averages. His best per-target game (56 yards on 5-of-10 targets) came against Kansas City's 4th-ranked WR defense in Week 3 — real production against a tough individual matchup.

**Caleb Douglas (MIA):** A true rookie (2 career games, no 2025 track record anywhere) who exploded for 94 yards on 5 catches (7 targets) in Week 1 against a middle-12 pass defense (Las Vegas, 15th) before going quiet in Week 2 (19 yards). A name worth monitoring given the per-target juice (16.14 yards per reception) even in a tiny sample.

**Greg Dulcich (MIA, TE1):** 69.3% snap share and a real red-zone role (25.0% red-zone target share). Season average of 30.0 rec yds/gm; his road average (relevant this week) and his exact tier-and-side combination for this matchup (a middle-12 tight end defense, Minnesota ranks 21st) are actually the same 17.5 rec yds/gm across the same two-game road sample — a rare case where the full four-level progression collapses to one number because his whole road history has come against middle-12 competition.

**Justin Jefferson (MIN):** The focal point of Minnesota's passing attack (25.4% target share, a massive 57.1% red-zone target share). Season average of 59.67 rec yds/gm is essentially identical to his 2025 pace (61.65 rec yds/gm) — a picture of remarkable stability rather than decline, despite his team's brutal overall passing numbers. His lone home game (Week 1 vs. Green Bay, a middle-12 pass defense) produced 92 yards on 8 catches and 2 touchdowns; no home sample yet exists against a top-10 WR defense, which is exactly the tier Miami's defense (8th against WRs specifically) represents this week — a genuinely difficult individual matchup on paper, even though Miami's overall pass defense ranks a mediocre 18th. Fall back to his season and home averages above given the gap.

**Jordan Addison (MIN):** 23.9% target share, with a strong Week 3 outing (90 yards on 5-of-9 targets, 1 TD) against Tampa Bay's 7th-ranked WR defense — real production against a legitimately tough individual matchup. His lone home game (Week 1) was a non-factor (0 catches, 2 targets); no home sample exists yet against a top-10 defense either, so his tiny single-game home average (0.0) should be treated as uninformative rather than predictive.

**T.J. Hockenson (MIN, TE1):** 73.3% snap share and a real red-zone role (28.6% red-zone target share). Season average of 25.33 rec yds/gm; his one home game (Week 1 vs. Green Bay's top-10 tight end defense) still produced 36 yards and a touchdown on 4-of-5 targets — solid production even against a tough individual assignment. This week represents a materially better matchup: Miami's tight end defense ranks 28th (bottom-10) by yardage allowed and, more specifically, has allowed the league's worst yards-per-reception mark to tight ends (14.0, 30th). No home sample against a bottom-10 tight end defense exists yet in his own splits, so fall back to season and home averages, but the raw opponent-quality gap here is one of the cleaner individual advantages in this report.

